## T4 · Dates and milestones

::: table | Topic 4 timeline.
@widths 1.8,12.2
@font 9.5
Year | Event
1817 | James Mill, *The History of British India* — women's status as the measure of civilisation
1829 | Bengal Sati Regulation
1856 | Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act
1860 | Indian Penal Code (s. 497 adultery)
1864–68 | Cantonment and Contagious Diseases Acts in India
1870 | Female Infanticide Prevention Act
1871 | Criminal Tribes Act (hijras)
1872 | Punjab Laws Act — custom first in inheritance
1884 | Engels, *The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State*
1891 | Age of Consent Act (10 → 12)
1927 | Katherine Mayo, *Mother India*
1929 | Child Marriage Restraint Act (Sarda Act)
1944 | World Bank founded (Bretton Woods)
1949 | Truman's "Point Four" speech (20 January); Murdock, *Social Structure*
1950 | Prebisch and ECLA — declining terms of trade
1955 | Parsons and Bales, *Family, Socialization and Interaction Process*
1958 | Lerner, *The Passing of Traditional Society*
1960 | Rostow, *The Stages of Economic Growth*
1966 | Frank, "The Development of Underdevelopment"
1968 | Mahbub ul Haq's "22 families"
1969 | Cardoso and Faletto, *Dependency and Development in Latin America*; Benston on housework
1970 | Boserup, *Woman's Role in Economic Development*
1972 | Rodney, *How Europe Underdeveloped Africa*; Dalla Costa and James
1973 | Percy Amendment (US)
1974 | USAID Office of WID; Wallerstein, *The Modern World-System*; NIEO at the UN; Oakley, *The Sociology of Housework*; Inkeles and Smith; d'Eaubonne coins "ecofeminism"
1975 | International Women's Year; Mexico City conference
1976 | Voluntary Fund for the UN Decade for Women (later UNIFEM)
1978 | IDS workshop on "The Continuing Subordination of Women"
1979 | CEDAW; Women's Division (Pakistan)
1982 | Mexico's debt default — the debt crisis
1983 | Fuentes and Ehrenreich, *Women in the Global Factory*; Grameen Bank
1984 | DAWN founded (Bangalore); Mohanty, "Under Western Eyes"
1985 | Molyneux, "Mobilization without Emancipation?"; Harvard Analytical Framework; Nairobi; Sen and Grown
1986 | Mies, *Patriarchy and Accumulation on a World Scale*
1987 | UNICEF, *Adjustment with a Human Face*
1988 | Waring, *If Women Counted*; Shiva, *Staying Alive*; Pakistan's first IMF adjustment programme
1989 | Williamson's "Washington Consensus"; Standing on feminisation of labour; Moser on gender needs; Commonwealth, *Engendering Adjustment*; Chatterjee on the nationalist resolution; First Women Bank (Pakistan)
1990 | UNDP Human Development Report (Haq); Sen, "Gender and Cooperative Conflicts"; Ward, *Women Workers and Global Restructuring*
1991 | Elson (ed.), *Male Bias in the Development Process*
1992 | Agarwal, "The Gender and Environment Debate"; Sachs (ed.), *The Development Dictionary*; Smith and Wallerstein, *Creating and Transforming Households*
1993 | Moser, *Gender Planning and Development*; Mies and Shiva, *Ecofeminism*
1994 | Kabeer, *Reversed Realities*
1995 | Beijing Platform — gender mainstreaming; UNDP GDI and GEM; WTO; Escobar, *Encountering Development*
1997 | ECOSOC definition of gender mainstreaming; Sialkot Atlanta Agreement
1999 | Kabeer, "Resources, Agency, Achievements"; Sen, *Development as Freedom*; PRSPs
2000 | Millennium Development Goals; Quijano on coloniality; Hochschild on global care chains; Kabeer, *The Power to Choose*; Nussbaum, *Women and Human Development*
2004 | Federici, *Caliban and the Witch*
2006 | WEF Global Gender Gap Index begins
2007 | Lugones on the coloniality of gender
2008 | Benazir Income Support Programme
2010 | UNDP Gender Inequality Index
2012 | World Development Report *Gender Equality and Development* ("smart economics"); Chant and Sweetman's critique; Ali Enterprises fire (Karachi, 11 September); National Climate Change Policy (Pakistan)
2013 | Rana Plaza collapse (24 April); Bangladesh Accord
2014 | EU GSP+ for Pakistan; UNFCCC Lima Work Programme on Gender
2015 | Sustainable Development Goals; Sendai Framework
2016 | Pakistan's National Assembly adopts the SDGs (February)
2017 | UNFCCC Gender Action Plan; Kathmandu South Asian meeting on SDGs and women
2018 | Sindh Home-Based Workers Act
2020 | Enforcement of Women's Property Rights Act
2021 | National Climate Change Policy updated
2022 | Floods (about 33 million affected); Climate Change Gender Action Plan; loss and damage fund (COP27)
2023 | IMF Stand-By Arrangement
2024 | IMF 7-billion-dollar Extended Fund Facility
:::

## T4 · The theories and approaches in one table

::: table | Theories and approaches — thinkers and core claims.
@widths 3.2,4.4,6.4
@font 9.5
Theory / approach | Key names (years) | Core claim about women
Modernisation | Rostow 1960; Lerner 1958 | Tradition oppresses; modernity frees (critique: Boserup 1970)
Dependency | Prebisch 1950; Frank 1966; Rodney 1972 | Largely silent; explains cheap labour of the periphery
World-system | Wallerstein 1974; Smith and Wallerstein 1992 | Households' unpaid work subsidises low wages; global assembly line
Structural functionalism | Parsons and Bales 1955; Murdock 1949 | Instrumental male and expressive female roles are "functional"
WID | Boserup 1970; Percy 1973 | Integrate women into development
WAD | IDS 1978 | Women integrated on unequal, exploitative terms
GAD | Molyneux 1985; Moser 1993; Kabeer 1994/1999; DAWN 1984 | Transform gender power relations; empowerment
Coloniality | Quijano 2000; Lugones 2007; Escobar 1995 | Colonial patterns of power continue in development
Ecofeminism / feminist environmentalism | d'Eaubonne 1974; Shiva 1988 / Agarwal 1992 | Women and nature dominated together / women's link to nature is material
:::

